✅ Okay — this version of readSerial() is much cleaner — but there’s one key reason why it’s not working properly with multiple headers for the same RT:

You’re only tracking one header pattern (headers) —
but for each RT, you actually have two valid headers:

RT1 → AC CA 1F 0A and AC CA 1F 0B

RT2 → AC CA 1F 0C and AC CA 1F 0D

RT3 → AC CA 1F 0E and AC CA 1F 0F

Your current code only checks one header at a time, so when the second header comes, it never matches → no data gets read.

🧠 The Correct Way:
You should:

Define both headers for the selected RT

Track the last 4 bytes from the serial stream

Check if it matches either of the two valid headers for that RT

Read payload accordingly

✅ Fixed readSerial() for two headers per RT
def readSerial():
    global serialData, ser, hexDecodedData, selected_tab, counter_value

    # Get both header patterns for the selected RT
    header_patterns = [bytes.fromhex(h) for h in RT_HEADERS[selected_tab]]  # e.g. ['acca1f0a','acca1f0b']
    buffer = b""

    try:
        while serialData:
            if ser.in_waiting > 0:
                # Read 1 byte and append to buffer
                buffer += ser.read(1)

                # Keep last 4 bytes only
                if len(buffer) > 4:
                    buffer = buffer[-4:]

                # Check if buffer matches any of the RT's headers
                for header in header_patterns:
                    if buffer == header:
                        print(f"✅ {selected_tab} HEADER {header.hex().upper()} FOUND! Reading data...")
                        payload = ser.read(1206)  # read your payload
                        hexDecodedData = header.hex() + payload.hex()
                        data_queue.put((selected_tab, hexDecodedData))
                        counter_value += 1
                        buffer = b""  # reset buffer after match
                        break

            time.sleep(0.001)

    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        print("Reading from serial port stopped")
